WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:01:39.440 --> 00:01:42.040
Please welcome Björn Ulvaeus!

00:01:56.000 --> 00:01:58.000
-Welcome.
-Thank you.

00:01:58.160 --> 00:02:00.720
Was that an actual rumour?

00:02:00.880 --> 00:02:06.240
I don't even remember that.
But it probably was.

00:02:06.400 --> 00:02:12.080
Because a lot of people went,
"what a gimmick, two married couples."

00:02:12.240 --> 00:02:16.560
A redhead and a blonde beauty
who could sing, to boot.

00:02:16.720 --> 00:02:19.200
It was too good to be true.

00:02:21.960 --> 00:02:28.000
Björn, you're throwing a party,
aren't you?

00:02:28.160 --> 00:02:31.880
-You're a bit of a party planner.
-Yes, I suppose.

00:02:32.040 --> 00:02:37.440
We saw some clips from&lt;i&gt;Mamma Mia!,&lt;/i&gt;
which was a huge hit.

00:02:37.600 --> 00:02:41.000
Now there's a sequel,
&lt;i&gt;Mamma Mia! The Party.&lt;/i&gt;

00:02:41.160 --> 00:02:44.680
What is "the party"?

00:02:44.840 --> 00:02:49.440
It came out of...
At the end of the stage musical,

00:02:49.600 --> 00:02:56.360
people stand up, sing and dance,
and there's sort of a party vibe.

00:02:56.520 --> 00:03:01.000
People sort of wanted
to keep that party going afterwards.

00:03:01.160 --> 00:03:05.160
That's the idea behind
&lt;i&gt;Mamma Mia! The Party.&lt;/i&gt;

00:03:05.320 --> 00:03:10.480
We take this atmosphere to
a restaurant setting, a Greek taverna.

00:03:10.640 --> 00:03:17.200
It's Nikos, the owner of the taverna,
and his friends and family.

00:03:17.360 --> 00:03:20.400
Between them,
a story takes place each night.

00:03:28.360 --> 00:03:32.520
But the songs are different
to those in&lt;i&gt;Mamma Mia!&lt;/i&gt;

00:03:32.680 --> 00:03:38.120
So, when it comes to looking after
the ABBA legacy, so to speak,

00:03:38.280 --> 00:03:41.240
you seem to be the most active
in that sense.

00:03:41.400 --> 00:03:45.120
It's mostly your responsibility,
like a big brother.

00:03:45.280 --> 00:03:48.880
It's probably a bit of a coincidence.

00:03:49.040 --> 00:03:53.720
I think it all began with&lt;i&gt;Mamma Mia!&lt;/i&gt;

00:03:53.880 --> 00:03:57.880
I really liked the idea of it.

00:03:58.040 --> 00:04:03.560
I saw the musical&lt;i&gt;Grease&lt;/i&gt;
with my children,

00:04:03.720 --> 00:04:07.600
and I had a vision of what it could be.

00:04:07.760 --> 00:04:14.520
Happy, cheerful fun for the whole family
with lots of hit songs.

00:04:14.680 --> 00:04:18.200
I thought, well,
I really need to do this,

00:04:18.360 --> 00:04:22.600
because I'm actually able
to make this happen.

00:04:22.760 --> 00:04:26.480
It was kind of an experiment
that I started.

00:04:26.640 --> 00:04:31.880
I was ready to abort it
if it wasn't panning out.

00:04:32.040 --> 00:04:36.960
Because the most important thing
was always the songs, and ABBA,

00:04:37.120 --> 00:04:40.120
that this wouldn't hurt them
in any way.

00:04:40.280 --> 00:04:44.600
Do you have to ask their permission
for every little thing you do?

00:04:44.760 --> 00:04:51.080
-You bet. I sure have to.
-Are they difficult...?

00:04:51.240 --> 00:04:54.560
Not for everything,
but for major things like this.

00:04:54.720 --> 00:04:57.040
-Are they difficult?
-Not at all.

00:04:57.200 --> 00:05:00.920
A couple of them had their doubts
about&lt;i&gt;Mamma Mia!&lt;/i&gt;

00:05:01.080 --> 00:05:03.880
but for this,
they all gave their go-ahead.

00:05:04.040 --> 00:05:08.240
The other three came
to the&lt;i&gt;Mamma Mia!&lt;/i&gt;premiere.

00:05:08.400 --> 00:05:13.720
-Will they come this time?
-If they'll come? They've been invited.

00:05:13.880 --> 00:05:19.240
And I guess we'll see...if they turn up.

00:05:21.280 --> 00:05:25.200
-It looks like you know they will come.
-Really?

00:05:25.360 --> 00:05:28.880
That's your interpretation.

00:05:29.040 --> 00:05:35.160
When you send out your invites
you ask for a reply, don't you?

00:05:35.320 --> 00:05:38.160
They need to reply, don't they?

00:05:38.320 --> 00:05:42.560
-Reply to...?
-You know, RSVP.

00:05:42.720 --> 00:05:48.320
Right, they've all RSVPed.
Yes. I can tell you that much.

00:05:48.480 --> 00:05:51.880
And are they nice RSVPs?

00:05:52.040 --> 00:05:56.280
They all write very nice
and clear RSVPs, yes.

00:06:00.840 --> 00:06:07.120
You've also been actively involved
with the ABBA museum.

00:06:09.920 --> 00:06:14.440
Are you a bit of a control freak
in that sense?

00:06:14.600 --> 00:06:19.120
No, I just wanted a museum
dedicated to me.

00:06:23.320 --> 00:06:25.400
No, I...

00:06:26.880 --> 00:06:31.800
No, it just suddenly dawned on me

00:06:31.960 --> 00:06:35.360
that a museum in Stockholm
was going to happen.

00:06:35.520 --> 00:06:43.160
I wanted to make sure the quality of it
would be the best I could make it,

00:06:43.320 --> 00:06:47.120
and that's why I became involved,
not because I wanted to.

00:06:47.280 --> 00:06:50.760
But once I did, it was rather fun.

00:06:50.920 --> 00:06:55.840
Because that young man
with the peculiar suits,

00:06:56.000 --> 00:06:59.480
he sort of became
this other person to me.

00:06:59.640 --> 00:07:03.880
A kind of "historical figure"
I could tell a story about.

00:07:04.040 --> 00:07:06.480
And that made things easier.

00:07:06.640 --> 00:07:13.080
You've said that you and Benny...

00:07:13.240 --> 00:07:16.880
...have got one more musical in you.

00:07:17.040 --> 00:07:21.160
I've said I think we do,
that we might have another one in us.

00:07:21.320 --> 00:07:23.920
Are you working on one?

00:07:24.080 --> 00:07:28.440
We've been...
I'm being very evasive again.

00:07:31.040 --> 00:07:35.000
We've got a few ideas in circulation.

00:07:35.160 --> 00:07:38.040
Right. Are they any good?

00:07:38.200 --> 00:07:42.560
If they're in circulation,
they're good ideas, I'll say that much.

00:07:42.720 --> 00:07:45.920
But nothing's been decided yet.

00:07:46.080 --> 00:07:51.600
What's the process like? You've made
a few of these musicals by now.

00:07:51.760 --> 00:07:56.600
-First you have to find a story?
-Yes. That's the main thing.

00:07:56.760 --> 00:07:59.760
And where do you find them,
in literature?

00:07:59.920 --> 00:08:06.040
In literature, in drama, in films...

00:08:06.200 --> 00:08:10.280
Or you could start from scratch,
like we did with&lt;i&gt;Chess.&lt;/i&gt;

00:08:10.440 --> 00:08:15.560
It doesn't matter where it comes from,
it's wherever you get the best idea.

00:08:15.720 --> 00:08:20.480
When you say that you may have
one musical left in you,

00:08:20.640 --> 00:08:25.680
it's a bit sad, because it's something
you say at a certain age, isn't it?

00:08:25.840 --> 00:08:27.160
Yes...

00:08:27.320 --> 00:08:31.040
And you're someone
who thinks a great deal about death.

00:08:33.080 --> 00:08:37.120
I used to think more about death
five years ago than I do now.

00:08:37.280 --> 00:08:39.320
How so?

00:08:39.480 --> 00:08:44.680
Well, I was 65 years old at the time.

00:08:44.840 --> 00:08:51.120
And it's not unusual
to worry about death.

00:08:51.280 --> 00:08:55.160
You're too young to understand
what I'm talking about.

00:08:56.480 --> 00:09:02.280
But as the horizon closes in on you,
you start thinking about it more.

00:09:02.440 --> 00:09:04.880
I think all people do.

00:09:06.200 --> 00:09:13.000
But...I believe it helps
to reflect on it a lot.

00:09:13.160 --> 00:09:17.840
It allows you to face the fact
that it is all coming to an end.

00:09:18.000 --> 00:09:24.080
Then you learn to live with it
and you think about it less.

00:09:24.240 --> 00:09:28.600
You're an outspoken atheist,
so the end, that's really it to you.

00:09:28.760 --> 00:09:30.720
Yes.

00:09:30.880 --> 00:09:38.640
You've been very actively talking
about wanting to die on your own terms.

00:09:38.800 --> 00:09:43.360
Euthanasia, to put it bluntly,
should be an option in your opinion.

00:09:43.520 --> 00:09:46.600
-Yes.
-What are your thoughts on that?

00:09:46.760 --> 00:09:51.680
Well, I've personally witnessed...

00:09:51.840 --> 00:09:56.240
...the final years of both my parents
and my parents-in-law.

00:09:56.400 --> 00:10:00.040
And they were difficult years.

00:10:00.200 --> 00:10:07.200
And I thought,
I definitely don't want to go that way.

00:10:08.400 --> 00:10:11.120
So I began thinking about euthanasia.

00:10:11.280 --> 00:10:15.560
And I think, in a country like Sweden,

00:10:15.720 --> 00:10:21.880
politicians ought to properly
look into how it could work.

00:10:22.040 --> 00:10:28.680
But they're not even willing to do
that much. They won't touch the issue.

00:10:28.840 --> 00:10:33.560
They're so afraid of it, even though
I've read that a majority of Swedes

00:10:33.720 --> 00:10:37.240
would be favourable
to some kind of euthanasia.

00:10:37.400 --> 00:10:44.280
You say the final years are difficult.
But isn't that just part of life?

00:10:44.440 --> 00:10:47.600
Yes, but it feels so pointless,

00:10:47.760 --> 00:10:52.920
seeing your close ones suffer
in the manner I've seen them do.

00:10:53.080 --> 00:10:55.640
It feels so utterly pointless.

00:10:55.800 --> 00:11:00.520
I think it would be comforting
for elderly people to know

00:11:00.680 --> 00:11:04.720
that you won't have to go that way.

00:11:04.880 --> 00:11:10.720
That you could be in charge of how
and when you wanted to end it.

00:11:10.880 --> 00:11:14.920
People who oppose that sort of thinking
tend to point out

00:11:15.080 --> 00:11:20.560
that elderly people often don't want
to be a burden on their families.

00:11:20.720 --> 00:11:26.800
That they might feel pressure
to accept a solution like that.

00:11:26.960 --> 00:11:32.520
Yes, it's hard to determine...
It's a complex and really tough issue.

00:11:32.680 --> 00:11:39.440
But it does work in many countries,
and has done for many years.

00:11:39.600 --> 00:11:42.000
Belgium, Netherlands, Switzerland...

00:11:42.160 --> 00:11:48.560
Not to mention the state of Oregon
in the United States.

00:11:48.720 --> 00:11:52.520
They've had euthanasia for 17 years.

00:11:52.680 --> 00:11:56.360
And a rather uncontroversial
version of it, in my view.

00:11:56.520 --> 00:12:00.160
I think you need
two independent medical opinions

00:12:00.320 --> 00:12:06.560
saying you've got a chronic condition
and less than six months to live.

00:12:06.720 --> 00:12:12.040
Then they'll prescribe something
that lets you end it whenever you wish.

00:12:12.200 --> 00:12:18.200
As a civilised, secular country,
we ought to be able to implement that.

00:12:19.960 --> 00:12:26.960
On tonight's show, amongst other topics,

00:12:27.120 --> 00:12:31.720
we're going to discuss perhaps
the biggest issue in Europe today,

00:12:31.880 --> 00:12:35.920
the situation for refugees
and the immigration issue.

00:12:36.080 --> 00:12:43.960
I know you've actively sought out
an immigrant friend.

00:12:44.120 --> 00:12:48.560
-Yes, that's right.
-You didn't know anyone before?

00:12:48.720 --> 00:12:53.640
No. We had partnered up
with the Red Cross

00:12:53.800 --> 00:13:00.200
during the Gothenburg Opera revival
of&lt;i&gt;Kristina from Duvemåla.&lt;/i&gt;

00:13:01.680 --> 00:13:06.920
We figured
an epic emigrant story like that

00:13:07.080 --> 00:13:11.240
could have a positive effect
on the whole debate.

00:13:11.400 --> 00:13:16.160
Then I learned the Red Cross promoted
something called&lt;i&gt;Kompis Sverige.&lt;/i&gt;

00:13:16.320 --> 00:13:20.960
It's for people like me,
because we live in a segregated society.

00:13:21.120 --> 00:13:25.320
And I realised I never get to meet
any of the new arrivals.

00:13:25.480 --> 00:13:31.400
I never get to hear what it's like
to come to Sweden that way.

00:13:31.560 --> 00:13:35.960
So I got in touch with&lt;i&gt;Kompis Sverige.&lt;/i&gt;

00:13:36.120 --> 00:13:41.960
They interviewed me to find out
about my interests and who I am.

00:13:42.120 --> 00:13:46.240
-And likewise with...
-I assume they knew who you were?

00:13:46.400 --> 00:13:49.760
Who you&lt;i&gt;really&lt;/i&gt;are.
It went a bit deeper than that.

00:13:49.920 --> 00:13:55.840
And they did likewise with new arrivals
and paired us up.

00:13:56.000 --> 00:14:00.720
And they paired me up with Issam,
who is Palestinian.

00:14:00.880 --> 00:14:04.000
He's been in Sweden for a few years.

00:14:04.160 --> 00:14:07.720
-He and I meet up...
-Is he your age, or younger...?

00:14:07.880 --> 00:14:11.720
He's in his sixties.

00:14:11.880 --> 00:14:16.440
We've met for lunch a couple of times
and have started to talk.

00:14:16.600 --> 00:14:20.320
I'll get him to speak more
in Swedish eventually.

00:14:20.480 --> 00:14:25.840
For now, it's mostly in English.
We're meeting again in a few weeks.

00:14:26.000 --> 00:14:29.280
-What do you talk about?
-Everything.

00:14:29.440 --> 00:14:35.480
Politics, religions, tradition, culture,
things I really want to know about.

00:14:35.640 --> 00:14:41.200
And I get a first-hand account
from someone who is from there.

00:14:41.360 --> 00:14:48.040
It must be peculiar for Issam, who was
meant to get a "typical Swedish buddy".

00:14:50.320 --> 00:14:54.480
-Exactly.
-Then it turns to be Björn from ABBA.

00:14:54.640 --> 00:14:59.440
Was the choice between you
and Ingvar Kamprad?

00:15:04.600 --> 00:15:07.880
I don't know
who the other candidates were.

00:15:08.040 --> 00:15:10.480
But he was a bit surprised.

00:15:10.640 --> 00:15:15.040
He didn't know who I was
when we first met.

00:15:15.200 --> 00:15:17.560
-So that was a...
-He didn't know?

00:15:17.720 --> 00:15:20.840
Oh, so when he met you
he realised it was you.

00:15:21.000 --> 00:15:25.680
-In the interviews, he had no idea.
-Right, it was a blind date.

00:15:25.840 --> 00:15:30.960
It was a typical blind date.
It's really dating, what we're doing.

00:15:31.120 --> 00:15:38.680
But it's a great, simple, tangible way
for everyone to engage.

00:15:38.840 --> 00:15:42.200
Anyone of us can make
a newly arrived friend

00:15:42.360 --> 00:15:47.480
and learn what it's like to come
to Sweden as an immigrant.

00:15:47.640 --> 00:15:52.480
Great to hear, great to have you here.
We'll have some more guests.

00:15:52.640 --> 00:15:56.640
&lt;i&gt;Mamma Mia! The Party&lt;/i&gt;premieres
in Stockholm later in January.

00:15:56.800 --> 00:15:59.400
-Thank you, Björn Ulvaeus.
-Thank you.

